[tua \n| starring       = [[Ingrid Bergman]]\n[[Tullio Carminati]] \n| music          = [[Arthur Honegger]]\n| cinematography = [[Gábor Pogány]]\n| editing        = [[Jolanda Benvenuti]]\n| distributor    =\n| released       = {{Film date|1954|12|20|df=y}}\n| runtime        = 76 minutes\n| country        = [[Italy]]\n| language       = [[Italian language|Italian]]\n| budget         =\n}}\n\n\u0022\u0022'Giovanna d'Arco al rogo\u0022\u0022' (English: \u0022Joan of Arc at the Stake\u0022) is a 1954 [[Italy|Italian]] film directed by [[Roberto Rossellini]] and starring his wife [[Ingrid Bergman]], which shows a live performance on December 1953 at the [[San Carlo Theatre]] in [[Naples]]. It is based on the oratorio [[Jeanne d'Arc au Bûcher]] by [[Paul Claudel]] and [[Arthur Honegger]]. It was filmed using a color process called Gevacolor.\n\n==Plot==\nThe film takes place mostly in a surrealistic fantasy around the time of the execution of [[Joan of Arc]]. Joan of Arc, played by Ingrid Bergman, is being burned alive for heresy. In a kind of dream state, she departs from her body and looks back upon her life. She begins this journey depressed and demoralized. However, a priest appears to help guide her. First, he shows her those who accused her in the guise of animal characters, in order to show her their true nature. Then, he shows her the good that she has performed for people. In the end, she is proud of what she has done and is ready to face the flames.\n\n==Reception==\nLike most Bergman and Rossellini collaborations, it did not perform well at the box office.\n\n== External links ==\n* {{IMDb title | id = 0047026 | title = Giovanna d'Arco al rogo}}\n\n{{Roberto Rossellini}}\n\n[[Category:1954 films]]\n[[Category:
 drama films]]\n[[Category:
 historical films]]\n[[Category:Italian epic films]]\n[[Category:Italian historical films]]\n[[Category:Italian films]]\n[[Category:Italian-language films]]\n[[Category:Films directed by Roberto Rossellini]]\n[[Category:Films about Joan of Arc]]\n[[Category:Films about Christianity]]\n[[Category:Religious epic films]]\n\n\n{{historic-film-stub}}\n{{
-Italy-film-stub}}</text>\n      <sha1>llluh6q0ka2u1jiw3mg8emomc061imp</sha1>\n    </revision>\n  </page>\n  <page>\n    <title>European Parliament election, 2009 (Italy)</title>\n    <ns>0</ns>\n    <id>11789225</id>\n    <revision>\n      <id>728428490</id>\n      <parentid>688380442</parentid>\n      <timestamp>2016-07-05T11:10:03Z</timestamp>\n      <contributor>\n        <username>BrownHairedGirl</username>\n        <id>754619</id>\n      </contributor>\n      <minor />\n      <comment>{{[[Template:European Parliament elections|European Parliament elections]]}} using [[Project:AWB|AWB]]</comment>\n      <model>wikitext</model>\n      <format>text/x-wiki</format>\n      <text xml:space=\u0022preserve\u0022>{{Cleanup|reason=English needs to be improved|date=September 2014}}\n\n{{Infobox Election\n| election_name      = Italian election of European Parliament, 2009\n| country            = Italy\n| flag_year          = \n| type               = parliamentary\n| ongoing            = \n| party_colour       = \n| previous_election  = European Parliament election, 2004 (Italy)\n| previous_year      = 2004\n| next_election      = European Parliament election, 2014 (Italy)\n| next_year          = 2014\n| seats_for_election = 72 seats to the [[European Parliament]]\n| election_date      = 6 and 7 June 2009\n| turnout            = \n| image1             = \n| leader1            = [[Silvio Berlusconi]]\n| leader_since1      = 26 January 1994\n| party_colour1      = blue\n| party1             = The People of Freedom\n| alliance1          = European People's Party\n| home_state1        = \n| leaders_seat1      = \n| last_election1     = 32,4%\n| seats_before1      = 25\n| electoral_vote1    = \n| states_carried1    = \n| seats1             = 29\n| seats_after1       = \n| seat_change1       = {{increase}}4\n| popular_vote1      = 10,797,296\n| percentage1        = 35.3%\n| swing1             = {{increase}}2.9%\n| image2
