{{flat list| \n* [[Psychedelic rock]]{{sfn|Greene|2010|p=156}}{{sfn|Shephard|Leonard|2013|p=182}} \n* [[art pop]]\n* [[acid rock]]{{sfn|Greene|2010|p=156}}{{sfn|Romano|2010|p=17}}\n* [[psychedelic pop]]{{sfn|Hoskyns|2009|p=128}}\n* [[avant-pop]]\n<!----\n* {{nowrap|[[avant-garde pop]]\n--->\n* [[rhythm and blues|R&B]]{{sfn|Hickey|2011|p=120}}{{sfn|Priore|2005|p=48}}\n}}\n| Length         = 
\n| Label          = [[Capitol Records|Capitol]]\n| Writer         = {{flatlist|\n* [[Brian Wilson]]\n* [[Mike Love]]}}\n| Producer       = Brian Wilson\n| Last single    = \u0022[[Wouldn't It Be Nice]]\u0022\n(1966)\n| This single    = \u0022\u0022'Good Vibrations\u0022'\u0022\n(1966)\n| Next single    = \u0022[[Then I Kissed Her]]\u0022\n(1967)\n| Misc           =  \n{{Extra track listing\n | Album      = [[Smiley Smile]]\n | Type       = album\n | prev_track = \u0022[[Little Pad]]\u0022\n | prev_no    = 5\n | this_track = \u0022\u0022'Good Vibrations\u0022'\u0022\n | track_no   = 6\n | next_track = \u0022[[With Me Tonight]]\u0022\n | next_no    = 7\n}}\n{{External music video|{{Youtube|apBWI6xrbLY|\u0022Good Vibrations\u0022}}}}\n{{Extra music sample\n  | title        = \u0022Good Vibrations\u0022\n  | filename     = Beach_Boys - Good_Vibrations.ogg\n  | Type         = single}}\n}}\n\n\u0022\u0022'Good Vibrations\u0022'\u0022 is a song composed and produced by [[Brian Wilson]] with words by [[Mike Love]] for the American [[rock music|rock]] band [[the Beach Boys]]. Released as a single in October 1966, it was an immediate critical and commercial hit, topping [[record chart]]s in several countries including the US and UK. Characterized by its complex soundscapes, episodic structure, and subversions of [[pop music]] formula, it was the most costly single ever recorded at the time of its release. \u0022Good Vibrations\u0022 later became widely acclaimed as one of the greatest masterpieces of rock music.{{sfn|Harrison|1997|p=34}}{{sfn|Candelaria|2014|p=130}}\n\nInitiated during the sessions for the album \u0022[[Pet Sounds]]\u0022 (1966), it was not taken from or issued as a lead single for an album, but rather as a stand-alone single, with the \u0022Pet Sounds\u0022 instrumental \u0022[[Let's Go Away for Awhile]]\u0022 as the B-side. It was envisioned for the unfinished album \u0022[[Smile (The Beach Boys album)|Smile]]\u0022, but instead appeared on the substitute LP \u0022[[Smiley Smile]]\u0022 (1967). Most of the song was developed as it was recorded. Its title derived from Wilson's fascination with [[Energy (esotericism)|cosmic vibrations]], after his mother once told him as a child that dogs sometimes bark at people in response to their \u0022bad vibrations\u0022. He used the concept
