[{Cadillac V8]]\n| transmission = \n| wheelbase    = \n| length       = \n| width        = \n| height       = \n| weight       = \n| aka          = \n| related      = None \n| designer     =\n}}\n\n\u0022\u0022'City of Industry\u0022\u0022' is a one of two [[Gasser (car)|gasser]]s sharing the name. \n\nThe first car named \u0022City of Industry\u0022 was a [[Cadillac]]-powered 1953 [[Kurtis Kraft|Kurtis]].  It  won the [[National Hot Rod Association|NHRA]] national titles in A/SP (A Production) at [[Oklahoma City, Oklahoma]] in 1958 (with a pass of 12.17 seconds at  122.44 mph ), AM/SP (A Modified Production) at [[Detroit Dragway]]  in 1960   (with a pass of 12.29  seconds at  130.62 mph ), and at [[Indianapolis Raceway Park]] in 1961 (with a pass of 11.91 seconds at  128.20 mph ) and 1962 (with a pass of 12.53 seconds at  111.80 mph ).\n\nThe second was a Cadillac-powered 1963 Kurtis.  It won the national AAM/SP (A Modified Production [[Supercharging|supercharged]]) title at [[Indianapolis Raceway Park]] in 1964 with a 10.62/ 132.93 mph  pass. \n\nBoth were driven by [[ Sam Parriott]] throughout their racing careers. \n\n==Notes==\n{{reflist}}\n\n==Sources==\n*Davis, Larry. \u0022Gasser Wars\u0022, North Branch, MN:  Cartech, 2003, pp.180-8.\n\n{{motorsports-stub}}\n{{automobile-stub}}\n\n\n[[Category:
 automobiles]]\n[[Category:
 automobiles]]\n[[Category:drag racing]]\n[[Category:Rear-wheel-drive vehicles]]\n\n{{DISPLAYTITLE:\u0022City of Industry\u0022 (car)}}</text>\n      <sha1>azqwo217adm210dn6dpst5nl1lvz0gv</sha1>\n    </revision>\n  </page>\n  <page>\n    <title>Portal 20 de Julio (TransMilenio)</title>\n    <ns>0</ns>\n    <id>53900786</id>\n    <revision>\n      <id>777724321</id>\n      <parentid>777713109</parentid>\n      <timestamp>2017-04-28T21:24:45Z</timestamp>\n      <contributor>\n        <username>CharlesT854</username>\n        <id>30920408</id>\n      </contributor>\n      <model>wikitext</model>\n      <format>text/x-wiki</format>\n      <text xml:space=\u0022preserve\u0022>{| class=\u0022infobox\u0022 style=\u0022width\u0022: 25em; font-size:90%;\u0022\n|-\n|colspan=\u00223\u0022 bgcolor=\u0022{{transmilenio/color|L}}\u0022 style=\u0022text-align: center; font-size: larger;\u0022 | \u0022'Portal 20 de Julio\u0022'\n|-\n|colspan=2|\n{| width=100%  style=\u0022background: #f9f9f9;\u0022\n|- style=\u0022vertical-align: center; text-align: center;\u0022\n\n<center>\n|- style=\u0022vertical-align: center; text-align: center;\u0022\n|width=40%|\u0022'[[Country Sur (TransMilenio)|Country Sur]]\u0022'\n|width=20%|◄ {{transmilenio|line=L}} ►\n|width=40%|\u0022Terminal\u0022\n|}\n|-\n|\u0022'Location\u0022'\n|Carrera 5A with Calle 31 Sur\n|-\n|\u0022'Neighborhood\u0022'\n|[[San Cristobal (Bogota)|San Cristóbal]]\n|- style=\u0022vertical-align: center;\u0022\n|\u0022'Opened\u0022'\n|September 15, 2012\n|- style=\u0022vertical-align: top; text-align: center;\u0022\n|}\nThe \u0022'Portal 20 de Julio\u0022' is one of the terminal or head stations that are part of the mass transit system of [[Bogota]],[[TransMilenio]] inaugurated in the year [[2000]]. It is located in the southeast of the city, on Carrera 5A between 30A South and 32 South. It has a simple pedestrian access on Calle 31 Sur.\n\n== Surroundings ==\nThe Portal receives for being the head station of the race line Tenth, and of the neighborhood where it is located. It is a cultural and religious landmark in the city of Bogota.\n\nServes the demand of the districts Suramérica,[[Veinte de Julio (Bogota)|Veinte de Julio]], San Isidro, Villa of the Alps I, Bello Ho
